  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nThe sun is shining and a look out the window (and most area \nwebcams) show the sky with a brilliant blue color. Temperatures\nhave climbed into the mid 60s under the sunshine with upper 60s\nexpected for the high temperature today. GOES satellite water \nvapor imagery shows an amplifying wave over the eastern Dakotas \nand west-central Minnesota presently. Showers and isolated \nstorms accompany the reflected surface front with the wave and \nare expected to track east through the overnight. The forecast \nleaned on the HRRR/RAP solutions (as they are doing a pretty \ngood job at current conditions in the northern Plains) which \nkeeps most, if not all, of the precipitation in Wisconsin \ntonight. A sliver of slight chance PoPs were kept in the \nnorthern tier counties just south of the Wisconsin state line, \nbut given the amount of dry air over Illinois, there is lower \nconfidence in sprinkles, much less a wetting rain. Soundings \nshow lapse rates aloft that are not terrible overnight, but \ninstability should weaken as the system crosses the Mississippi \nRiver keeping the risk for lightning at bay. The system should \nmostly pass over harmlessly, switching southeast winds to the \nnorthwest behind the front and ushering in a cooler, drier air \nmass.\n\nAny cloud cover that moves over northern Illinois tonight with the \nsystem should help quell any localized shallow fog chances tomorrow \nmorning. As winds turn to the northwest, temperatures will fall \ninto the low 40s, with chances near the Rockford Metro of \ntouching the upper 30s (though the previously mentioned clouds \ncould help prevent that). Given the northerly flow expected \ntomorrow, high temperatures were nudged down slightly; but with \ncloud cover decreasing through the day, temperatures should once\nagain return to the upper 60s with areas along and south of \nI-88 reaching the low 70s. Clearer skies are expected tomorrow \nnight which will help drop temperatures back down to the low 40s\nwith rural areas having a better chance at touching the upper \n30s.\n\nSeasonable temperatures will prevail through the week. High pressure \nwill help maintain mostly drier conditions, although another \nwave over the northern Plains heading to Wisconsin mid-week \nwill be monitored. Guidance continues to favor the track of the \nsystem north of Illinois so, for now, no precipitation is in the\nforeseeable future.\n\nDK",
